---
description: Widget/unit test conventions — bloc_test for blocs, pumpWidget with BlocProvider overrides, golden tests where applicable, test behavior not implementation
globs: ["**/*_test.dart"]
alwaysApply: false
---

## What This Rule Does

Applies to `_test.dart` files. Keeps tests asserting observable behavior
(rendered output, navigation, the state sequence a bloc emits) rather than
internal implementation details that break on harmless refactors.

> **Divergence from the upstream platform.** `flutter-platform` ships this
> rule in a Riverpod form (`ProviderScope` overrides, `ProviderContainer`).
> This app uses BLoC — see the note in `02-flutter-state-guard.mdc`.

## Bloc Tests: `bloc_test` and the Emitted State Sequence

A bloc's contract is *the sequence of states it emits in response to an
event*. Assert exactly that, with `bloc_test`:

```dart
void main() {
  late MockMenuRepository repository;

  setUp(() {
    repository = MockMenuRepository();
  });

  group('MenuBloc', () {
    blocTest<MenuBloc, MenuState>(
      'emits [inProgress, success] when the menu loads',
      setUp: () {
        when(() => repository.fetchMenu()).thenAnswer((_) async => [latte]);
      },
      build: () => MenuBloc(repository: repository),
      act: (bloc) => bloc.add(const MenuRequested()),
      expect: () => [
        const MenuLoadInProgress(),
        MenuLoadSuccess(drinks: [latte]),
      ],
    );

    blocTest<MenuBloc, MenuState>(
      'emits [inProgress, failure] when the repository throws',
      setUp: () {
        when(() => repository.fetchMenu())
            .thenThrow(const NetworkException('offline'));
      },
      build: () => MenuBloc(repository: repository),
      act: (bloc) => bloc.add(const MenuRequested()),
      expect: () => [
        const MenuLoadInProgress(),
        const MenuLoadFailure('offline'),
      ],
    );
  });
}
```

- **The failure path is not optional.** Every bloc test group covers at least
  one success and one failure sequence; a bloc tested only on the happy path
  is untested where it matters.
- `expect:` compares states by value — this is why every state extends
  `Equatable` (`02-flutter-state-guard.mdc`). If an `expect:` fails with two
  states that look identical, a missing field in `props` is the usual cause,
  not a bloc bug.
- Use `seed:` to start a bloc from a specific state rather than dispatching a
  chain of setup events.
- Use `wait:`/`skip:` sparingly and never to paper over a nondeterministic
  emit order — fix the bloc instead.
- Assert on emitted **states**, never on a bloc's private fields or on how
  many times a private helper ran.

## Widget Tests: `pumpWidget` + a Provided Bloc

Every widget test for a bloc-consuming widget provides that bloc explicitly —
never lets a real repository-backed bloc run in a widget test.

For most screens, provide a real bloc with a faked repository, which exercises
the actual state transitions:

```dart
testWidgets('shows the drinks returned by the repository', (tester) async {
  final repository = FakeMenuRepository();

  await tester.pumpWidget(
    RepositoryProvider<MenuRepository>.value(
      value: repository,
      child: MaterialApp(
        home: BlocProvider(
          create: (_) => MenuBloc(repository: repository)
            ..add(const MenuRequested()),
          child: const MenuScreen(),
        ),
      ),
    ),
  );

  await tester.pumpAndSettle();

  expect(find.text('Latte'), findsOneWidget);
});
```

When a test needs to pin the widget to one exact state (an error view, an
empty view), use `MockBloc` from `bloc_test` with a stubbed state instead —
it is the tool for asserting *rendering*, decoupled from transitions:

```dart
class MockMenuBloc extends MockBloc<MenuEvent, MenuState>
    implements MenuBloc {}

testWidgets('shows a retry button on failure', (tester) async {
  final bloc = MockMenuBloc();
  when(() => bloc.state).thenReturn(const MenuLoadFailure('offline'));

  await tester.pumpWidget(
    MaterialApp(
      home: BlocProvider<MenuBloc>.value(value: bloc, child: const MenuScreen()),
    ),
  );

  expect(find.text('Try again'), findsOneWidget);
});
```

- Register fallback values for any event type passed to `verify`/`when` with
  `registerFallbackValue` in `setUpAll` — `mocktail` requires it for
  non-primitive types.
- `pumpAndSettle()` after actions that trigger animations or async state
  changes; plain `pump()` when asserting an intermediate (e.g. in-progress)
  state before it resolves.
- A screen with all four states (initial/in-progress, success, empty success,
  failure) gets a test per state — that's the coverage bar for a generated
  screen, not just the populated case.

## Golden Tests

Where the repo already has golden tests set up (a `goldens/` directory and
`matchesGoldenFile` usage present), add a golden test for new visually
significant widgets that have no existing coverage — a card, a custom button
variant, an empty/error state illustration. Do not introduce golden testing to
a repo that doesn't already have it without asking; it's a noticeably
different CI/tooling commitment (font loading, platform-specific rendering)
that the team should opt into deliberately.

## Test Behavior, Not Implementation

- Assert what the user sees or what the app does (`find.text(...)`,
  `find.byIcon(...)`, navigation to a new route, a repository method called
  with the right arguments) — not a bloc's private field or an internal
  method's call count beyond what proves the behavior.
- Verifying a dispatched event is legitimate when the event *is* the
  behavior under test (a button dispatching `CartItemRemoved`):
  `verify(() => bloc.add(const CartItemRemoved('id-1'))).called(1);`

## Mocking

Use `mocktail` (already a dev dependency — confirm before reaching for
anything else, per `10-evidence-and-dependency-guard.mdc`) for repository
fakes, and `bloc_test`'s `MockBloc`/`MockCubit` for blocs. Prefer a small
hand-written fake over a mock when the interface is small and the test
benefits from readable, explicit behavior — the `Fake*Repository`
implementations in `lib/features/*/data/` are usable directly in tests.

## Integration Tests

End-to-end flows (sign-in, checkout) that need a real device/simulator use
the `integration_test` package, kept separate from `test/` (typically
`integration_test/`) so they don't run as part of the fast unit/widget suite
by default.

## Relationship to Other Rules

`02-flutter-state-guard.mdc` governs how state is structured and emitted;
this rule governs how that state is faked and asserted on in tests.
